# PART 3 — COMPLETE PRODUCT SEARCH OPTIMIZATION

## 12. Current requirement

Rebuild and optimize the existing product search functionality.

The search must work reliably with the real BSTORE catalog and dynamically reflect products added or edited through the Admin Panel.

Do not use hardcoded sample products.

Do not build a separate search catalog that becomes outdated.

## 13. Search fields

Search across all useful real product information:

- Product name.
- Brand name.
- Brand ID/reference where relevant.
- Model.
- Category name.
- Product type.
- Description.
- Search keywords.
- SKU or product code, if available.
- Relevant product attributes.

Support Georgian and English search terms.

Support common informal product names and abbreviations where practical.

Examples:

- Hoco headphones.
- Hoco ყურსასმენები.
- Borofone charger.
- Marshall speaker.
- Samsung charger.
- iPhone cable.
- Type-C კაბელი.
- ყურსასმენი.
- დამტენი.
- მაუსი.

A search for Hoco should find relevant Hoco products even when Hoco is stored as a separate brand reference.

## 14. Search normalization

Implement consistent normalization for:

- Uppercase/lowercase.
- Extra spaces.
- Leading/trailing spaces.
- Georgian and English text.
- Common punctuation differences.
- Product model formatting.
- Common spelling variations where practical.

Do not damage the original display text.

Use normalized values for searching and indexing, while preserving the original product name and brand display name.

## 15. Search relevance and ranking

Results should be ranked sensibly.

Suggested priority:

1. Exact product name match.
2. Exact brand + product name match.
3. Exact model match.
4. Brand match.
5. Category/product type match.
6. Description and keyword match.

Do not display unrelated results just because a generic character appears in their description.

Preserve the existing Catalog product cards and navigation.

## 16. Search performance

Inspect the current Firestore queries and indexing strategy.

Use appropriate filtering, pagination, and indexes.

Avoid downloading the entire product database repeatedly for every keystroke.

Use debouncing for search input.

Avoid unnecessary repeated network requests.

Handle loading, empty, and error states.

Do not show stale search results after a new query is submitted.

If Firestore cannot provide the desired full-text search efficiently, implement a realistic solution using the current Firebase architecture.

Do not claim that Firestore supports arbitrary full-text search natively.

Do not introduce an expensive external search service without explaining the need and cost.

For smaller catalogs, an appropriately maintained searchable product index or carefully designed client-side filtering may be sufficient.

Ensure newly added products become searchable after successful persistence.

Ensure edited product names, brands, and descriptions update the searchable data.

